Semaphore Sotto
Ingredients: COOL & WEST COAST JAZZ + TRIPPY, SURREAL & PSYCHEDELIC + Percussion + 1980s ERA
Creative Catalyst: It began as protest music performed on hacked traffic infrastructure, wiring instruments directly into municipal transit relays to weaponize psychedelic modal jams against urban gridlock.

A breezy, hallucinatory collision where laid-back Pacific cool jazz collides with manic 1980s digital sequencing and street-level kinetic percussion. Melodic flugelhorns drift like sea fog over polyrhythmic cadences generated by tapped pneumatic road hoses, rototoms, and glistening FM synthesis, evoking an alternate 1980s California where the freeways were unplugged and turned into surrealist open-air amphitheaters.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 76–96 BPM. An aloof, asymmetrical West Coast swing layered beneath polyrhythmic percussion storms, mimicking the unpredictable click-clack rhythms of traffic signal switches and automated pedestrian relays.
- Harmonic Palette: Lydian and Dorian modal jazz, lush major-ninth and suspended-eleventh chords, warped by psychedelic analog tape flutter, microtonal FM detuning, and chorus-drenched electric bass.
- Production Signatures: Gated-reverb brushwork running through vintage Lexicon 224 digital reverbs; subterranean sub-bass thuds triggered by hacked vehicle-axle sensors; dry, intimate muted trumpet lines smeared across modulated analog bucket-brigade delays.
- Dynamics: Impossibly chill, smoky restraint that suddenly fractures into blinding, kaleidoscopic percussion breaks—recreating the surreal shock of a green light flashing violently in thick ocean fog before dissolving back into stillness.

Scene & Origin Lore
Between 1984 and 1987, radical urbanists, art-school dropouts, and dissident jazz musicians in coastal California began prying open the locked aluminum "brainboxes" of municipal traffic gantries along Pacific Coast Highway. Dubbing themselves Relay Weavers, they jacked homemade step-sequencers and Yamaha DX7s into the terminal strips, transforming intersections into synchronized light-and-sound traps. Drivers trapped in artificial red lights were treated to impromptu midnight concerts: players clad in pastel linen suits draped with retroreflective highway vests performed atop pickup-truck beds, playing muted horns and rototoms while hacked signals pulsed surreal ambers and violets across the asphalt. The subculture celebrated The Slow Down—a ritual consumption of cold street food, citrus wine, and communal horn improv that ground civic hyper-efficiency to a sublime, smoky halt.

Typical Instruments: Muted flugelhorn, cool-toned alto saxophone, vibraphone, Yamaha DX7, Roland D-50, rototoms, agogô bells, brushed snare drums, pneumatic road tubes wired to analog trigger modules, and customized relay-solenoid rattles.

For Fans Of: Gerry Mulligan, Jon Hassell, Arthur Russell, late-era Talk Talk

